#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502169} :activity/id #uuid "eb6c5be4-36dc-53c7-bba2-d203e8249edd" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4683baa-7e31-565a-90dd-81bc97617456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402004} {:answer/id #uuid "e66e39bf-af21-54ee-9c01-1e665e79afcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402005} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb3bda1-f364-5e06-b3f9-1ec1294cdafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402006} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae71a29-b56a-5e44-b3f7-481f0faef4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402007} {:answer/id #uuid "e81114b5-b5d4-5ed1-9aad-cfda69edeb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce793d72-97c8-5680-9b23-e9cde7d661bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1fb0889d-6fed-5243-aaf7-929089854f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31954" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15ba228c-73d6-5b27-b06b-8123523bb9ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502183 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28523b60-9c94-5e08-8811-eeb6c2c8385b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260229} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6f925d-2385-5203-beed-2ae656f27e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260554} {:answer/id #uuid "71ffe7bc-2035-50b5-afb6-f098f001c957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260872} {:answer/id #uuid "c1024317-bb4d-5e9d-b670-c092e80227c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261578} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3f903-0607-5523-bb23-e9a27fc580dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260229} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e71e846-7ac8-5f2e-bf8c-340173700a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1298261d-fd1d-57fd-8c0d-689d1739fab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127888" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩=\\sqrt{2}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩=\\sqrt{2}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,2,-1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,{t}_{2},\\,3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "62869702-446a-5229-b3ad-6f5f53a978ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502185 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70033240-b70d-5bd1-a4ed-4790885b2931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385431} {:answer/id #uuid "178a3520-0f80-5c41-83eb-f447ec674bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385432} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f49b63-abb6-55d5-bfcd-031cbf531d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385433} {:answer/id #uuid "b22c45cb-175f-556e-9bfb-acf15dfc2a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385434} {:answer/id #uuid "6867b040-dbed-5cea-b52b-494baac4f0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385432} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b2cf6c5-f5c6-5493-95a1-8c32ef657814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08d3c76b-0a8e-5160-a10d-28283569af91" :question/math-academy-id "q-42492" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=8.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $2.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =2det(A) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "68c78553-6e54-5eb2-8283-0a51eda5ae73"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eacfc39-44f1-50b6-9661-06b34f1cc555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.922" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500664} {:answer/id #uuid "9223e8c1-ab98-539b-8995-df357364984e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.887" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500767} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d2f48f-f0a8-5a20-bf7d-5b6b11786401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.907" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500887} {:answer/id #uuid "d6451578-ea42-5bae-aefd-02f4a378e3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.677" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500926} {:answer/id #uuid "e91a7731-a4f2-5c1c-ab3c-3264404a0e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.932" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500887} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8ed450-14d6-56b8-9f85-42f0c5090225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7f6be4fe-78ba-5d67-91ec-fd3e20928e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131278" :question/problem "A supplier of clothing materials looks for flaws before selling the material to customers. On average, there have been $1.7$ flaws per meter. What is the probability of finding fewer than $4$  flaws in the next meter length of the material? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that flaws appear independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of flaws in a meter length of the material. Since the average rate is $1.7$ flaws per meter, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of flaws can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =1.7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(1.7).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{1.7}^{x}{e}^{-1.7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $4$  flaws in the next meter length of the material is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1.7}^{0}{e}^{-1.7}}{0!}+\\frac{{1.7}^{1}{e}^{-1.7}}{1!}+\\frac{{1.7}^{2}{e}^{-1.7}}{2!}+\\frac{{1.7}^{3}{e}^{-1.7}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.907\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "d76fac64-e76c-578f-8d90-9a9c135ee09b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b030142-b351-5e7f-9981-3a7a900479a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339506} {:answer/id #uuid "a7085a93-27ed-53b5-a303-a8fca730844d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340645} {:answer/id #uuid "b4affd08-9db3-5c2c-982f-d4b5ce542718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340767} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcdc6a7-206d-5669-bb3f-7c2d9331e8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340934} {:answer/id #uuid "df5e0a81-5e66-52e1-bec5-63012c9a7c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340645} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb292a0b-9d7e-5720-a326-a1c46c9f7bf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d5c520f5-835e-5cc8-a0fb-53c3d5239f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74597" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k+6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k+6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+4.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+4\\,⇒\\,k=-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dcb06b0e-9cf9-5cc0-bc6b-4c05909a7d23" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502183}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cce534e-4802-5d94-97bf-80beed889e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435976} {:answer/id #uuid "a997e5ce-81f8-5baf-8303-e27befaf4b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435984} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1bccd5-410f-5b91-93ca-dbe4d676607f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435987} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c7e480-2dae-55a9-9956-216be43bcfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435992} {:answer/id #uuid "f5361e88-3d20-5f4f-860f-c82dceb66009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435984} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee9d609-cf3a-562f-9f5d-2dd12fbb70da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eff26da6-299d-5b1f-93b8-02b4dbf809cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109064" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[7X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{4}{7}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[7X] & ={7}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =49\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=3.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}+4\\cdot \\frac{2}{7}+16\\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{7}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[7X] & =49\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =49\\cdot \\frac{10}{7} \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e77cc344-9a7e-5bda-bdd0-26ec1de9edf3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502185}}] :activity/title "Quiz 3" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501461}]}
